    "created_at": "2020-08-12T16:00:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-08-12T16:00:27Z",
    "body": "A few points:\r\n\r\n1. These delta values are fairly small, and there isn't an obviously correct choice of delta. The current value (1E-10) was chosen as it was lower than the deltas that I encountered when recompiling the codebase using different compilers on the same platform. It may be that the math routines in the new platform experience a bit more variation than I have previous seen, and that the correct response is to change epsilon to a larger value.\r\n2. I'd be uncomfortable with suggesting that folks use this branch / platform until someone roots around the code and find any places where \"long ints\" were used for protection from overflows in calculations. In this platform \"long int\" means \"int32_t\", but in the development environment it meant \"int64_t\". There surely exist such instances, and in these instances, the \"long int\" type should be changed to uint64_t and int64_t, depending on the context. Similarly, I'm curious as to the precision of double vs long double on the platform (this can be determined by just printing DBL_EPSILON and LDBL_EPSILON). If some sort of extended precision type isn't available, it may not advisable to perform calculations on this platform at all.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
